WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.343
Hi, I'm Tenon!

00:00:02.343 --> 00:00:06.203
MegaBake takes a PyTorch model and bakes a whole decode step

00:00:06.203 --> 00:00:07.607
into one GPU kernel.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:09.011
Here's how that works.

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:14.762
A small model at batch one spends most of each token launching kernels.

00:00:14.762 --> 00:00:18.248
So launch once, and let blocks that never exit walk a

00:00:18.248 --> 00:00:19.516
table of every step.

00:00:21.342 --> 00:00:25.678
Dynamo captures the model, and Inductor's own passes clean up the graph.

00:00:25.678 --> 00:00:28.568
MegaBake takes that graph and stops Inductor there.

00:00:28.988 --> 00:00:32.393
It groups the operations into regions, picks a body for each, and

00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:35.797
writes every step of the token as a row in one table.

00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:39.785
One kernel launches sixty blocks, one per processor, and they all

00:00:39.785 --> 00:00:41.083
walk the same table.

00:00:41.503 --> 00:00:45.605
Each row names a body: a matrix tile, a norm, or attention.

00:00:45.605 --> 00:00:49.366
A grid barrier closes each interval before the next rows begin.

00:00:50.692 --> 00:00:54.325
The usual way launches hundreds of kernels per token, and at batch

00:00:54.325 --> 00:00:56.747
one the host's launch time dwarfs the work.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:58.213
MegaBake launches once.

00:00:58.213 --> 00:01:02.395
Blocks synchronize with barriers, and tiny steps like norms are recomputed by

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.880
every block, so they need no barrier of their own.

00:01:07.092 --> 00:01:08.590
The repo's own numbers.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:12.711
End to end, the single launch wins by about five times.

00:01:12.711 --> 00:01:17.207
But on GPU time alone, Inductor's kernels are slightly faster, and its

00:01:17.207 --> 00:01:19.080
baseline ran without CUDA graphs.

00:01:20.642 --> 00:01:22.896
So the win today is launch overhead.

00:01:22.896 --> 00:01:26.759
To beat CUDA graphs too, a megakernel has to overlap work across

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:29.657
its barriers, and that is next on the roadmap.

00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:32.692
Graph in, one kernel out.

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:34.192
Sixty blocks walk a table.

00:01:34.192 --> 00:01:36.891
Faster end to end, not yet on the GPU.

00:01:37.311 --> 00:01:39.412
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.412 --> 00:01:40.012
I'm Tenon.

00:01:40.012 --> 00:01:40.312
Bye!
